{%- doc -%}
  BMP size-guide trigger, rendered inside the variant picker's option legend
  (`Size: M … Size Guide`) by the contracted edit in variant-main-picker —
  the design's in-row placement.

  The size guide's own presentation (link text, icon) lives on
  the "BMP: Size guide" block, but the OPTION NAME does not: it stays the theme
  setting `settings.bmp_size_guide_option` ("BMP: Size" panel), because the
  server-side add-to-cart gate reads the same value from blocks/buy-buttons and
  blocks/accelerated-checkout, where no block's settings are reachable, and on
  templates that carry no size-guide block at all. One naming setting serves
  both features (decision D2).

  So this snippet gates itself SERVER-SIDE on that name, as it always did: one
  button, in the one legend whose option matches, present in the first response.
  The two values it cannot read from here — the block's link text and icon
  toggle — leave the block by two different routes. The LABEL is the only thing
  JS touches: it ships with its shipped default already rendered, and
  assets/bmp-size-guide-portal.js swaps it only on stores that customised it,
  reading data-bmp-size-guide-label off the dialog. The ICON never crosses into
  JS: the block emits its own CSS rule for it, so it is right in the first paint
  and right with JS unavailable.

  The reveal stays pure CSS and is emitted by the block too, per PRODUCT:

    .bmp-size-guide__trigger[data-bmp-size-guide-product="<id>"] {
      display: inline-flex;
    }

  from a Liquid `style` tag inside the dialog-component (only that tag renders
  Liquid; a `stylesheet` block cannot carry a product id). This file's own
  sheet only hides. Two consequences it is worth being explicit about:

    - The product can have the configured option on a template with no
      size-guide block, and AC3 requires that to render nothing. No block, no
      rule, no trigger.
    - A page can hold a SECOND product's trigger — sections/featured-product
      .liquid and featured-product-information.liquid render a variant picker
      for a merchant-chosen product — or another product's dialog, adopted into
      <body> by the portal on quick-add. The old guard,
      `body:has([data-bmp-size-guide-dialog])`, was per document and revealed
      those too: a visible button whose click resolves nothing.

  In the theme editor only (request.design_mode), the same matching legend also
  carries a warning span: "size guide hidden — add the block". Because the name
  is server-side readable, the warning follows the merchant's real axis name
  rather than the literal "Size" — a store whose option is "Talla" is warned too,
  which is exactly the population that needs the warning. It is CSS-suppressed by the
  presence of a [data-bmp-size-guide-config] marker, which the block emits on
  its dialog, so the warning shows exactly when the template has no size-guide
  block. Live storefront: the span is never rendered — zero bytes.

  Opening is delegated to assets/bmp-size-guide-portal.js, NOT to a
  `on:click="#id/showDialog"` binding, for two reasons:

    - Horizon resolves an `on:click` `#id` target with
      document.querySelector (assets/component.js:265-268) — first match in
      document order. Quick-add clones the product page into its popup
      (assets/quick-add.js), so that can be the wrong copy of the id.
    - The chart must not open while it is a DOM descendant of the open
      quick-add dialog: the nested dialog's clicks/Escape bubble into the outer
      component's handlers, and `.quick-add-modal`'s forwards-filled
      transform makes it the containing block of the nested fixed-position
      dialog. The portal moves the node to <body> first — and the block's
      `style` tag rides inside that node, which is what makes the trigger
      visible inside the popup.

  An earlier fix suppressed this trigger inside `.quick-add-modal` with a
  display:none rule, which removed the feature from that surface instead of
  fixing it.

  The same module also owns the relocated-trigger case: a swatch app that renders its own
  UI and sets `display: none` inline on the whole `<variant-picker>` takes this
  trigger down with it, since the trigger renders inside that element's legend.
  CSS cannot reach a descendant of `display: none`, so the portal moves the node
  out of the picker and adds `bmp-size-guide__trigger--relocated` (rule below).

  @param {string} option_name - the variant option this legend belongs to
  @param {number} product_id - the product whose size-guide dialog this trigger opens
  @param {string} product_url - that product's page URL; the portal's fallback
    source for the dialog markup on mobile quick-add, where updateQuickAddModal
    drops `.product-details` and the cloned dialog with it
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign bmp_sg_option = settings.bmp_size_guide_option | strip | default: 'Size'
-%}
{%- if option_name == bmp_sg_option -%}
  <button
    type="button"
    class="bmp-size-guide__trigger button-unstyled"
    data-bmp-size-guide-product="{{ product_id }}"
    data-bmp-size-guide-url="{{ product_url }}"
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      No `hidden` and no Liquid gate: the icon is decided in CSS, by the second
      arm of the same per-product rule the block emits for the reveal, so both
      flip in one style recalculation, at parse time, with no JS — see the
      stylesheet.

      The SVG therefore ships on every PDP that has the configured option, even
      when the merchant turned the icon off: 752 bytes, once per response. That
      is the priced cost of keeping the toggle on the block, where the ticket
      wanted it. Omitting the markup server-side would require the trigger to
      read a block setting, which Liquid does not allow from this snippet.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <span
      class="svg-wrapper bmp-size-guide__trigger-icon"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >
      {{- 'bmp-icon-ruler.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}
    </span>
    {%- comment -%}
      Filled server-side with the shipped default so the trigger is complete in
      the first response and survives JS being unavailable. The portal replaces
      the text only when the block's own label differs, so the swap is reachable
      only on stores that customised it — and it is a text change inside a
      button already in its final position, not an appearance.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <span class="bmp-size-guide__trigger-label">{{- 'bmp.content.bmp_size_guide_default_label' | t -}}</span>
  </button>
  {%- if request.design_mode -%}
    <span class="bmp-size-guide__editor-warning">{{ 'bmp.content.bmp_size_guide_missing_block' | t }}</span>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{% stylesheet %}
  /* Hidden until THIS product's own size-guide dialog is in the document. The
     reveal is not here and cannot be: a static sheet renders no Liquid, so the
     strongest thing it could say is "this document holds some size-guide
     dialog" — and a document can hold another product's dialog (quick-add
     adopts one into <body>) or another product's trigger
     (featured-product{,-information} render a variant picker for a
     merchant-chosen second product). That document-wide guard is what shipped
     before, and it revealed triggers whose click resolves nothing.

     blocks/bmp-size-guide.liquid emits the matching reveal instead, from a
     Liquid `style` tag inside its dialog-component (spelled out rather than
     written as a tag, because theme check's StaticStylesheetAndJavascriptTags
     reads this comment as CSS and a real tag here is an error):

       .bmp-size-guide__trigger[data-bmp-size-guide-product="<id>"] {
         display: inline-flex;
       }

     — (0,2,0) against this rule's (0,1,0), so it wins with no `!important`
     — which ships exactly when and where the dialog ships:
     server-side, first response, per product, and travelling with the node
     when the portal adopts it into <body>. Still pure CSS: no class applied by
     JS stands between the server response and a visible trigger, and the
     product that has the configured option on a template with no size-guide
     block still renders nothing (AC3).

     The other declarations live here rather than in that per-product rule
     deliberately: they are inert while the button is `display: none`, so
     keeping them in the static, cacheable sheet leaves exactly one
     interpolated declaration per product on the page. */
  .bmp-size-guide__trigger {
    display: none;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--gap-2xs);
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: var(--font-size--sm);
  }

  /* The icon toggle is per-block config, and it is resolved in CSS, not in JS —
     by the second arm of that same per-product `style` tag, which the block
     emits only when its setting is on. So the icon and the reveal flip on the
     same fact, in the same style recalculation: both correct in the first
     paint and correct with JS unavailable. A JS-applied `hidden` could only
     ever arrive a frame later, and a 24.8px icon appearing inside a button
     pinned by `margin-inline-start: auto` is a visible jump on every product
     page at the shipped default.

     Descendant form, not the bare class: `.svg-wrapper` sets
     `display: inline-flex` at (0,1,0), so a bare `.bmp-size-guide__trigger-icon`
     rule would tie it and depend on sheet order. This (0,2,0) beats it, and the
     block's (0,3,0) beats this.

     Per PRODUCT, deliberately. It used to be
     `body:has([data-bmp-size-guide-icon])`, which meant a quick-add popup over
     a PDP — two dialogs in <body> — showed the popup product's icon whenever
     the HOST's toggle was on. */
  .bmp-size-guide__trigger .bmp-size-guide__trigger-icon {
    display: none;
  }

  /* Lay the legend out as one row — [Name: value ……… trigger] — only in the
     legend that actually holds the trigger. Since the option gate is back on
     the server there is exactly one such legend per product. */
  .variant-option legend:has(.bmp-size-guide__trigger) {
    display: flex;
    width: 100%;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .variant-option legend .bmp-size-guide__trigger {
    margin-inline-start: auto;
  }

  /* Relocated state. assets/bmp-size-guide-portal.js moves the
     trigger out of a <variant-picker> that a swatch app has hidden, into the
     picker's own parent — a theme container, typically a column flex panel.
     The in-legend `margin-inline-start: auto` above is scoped to the legend and
     correctly stops applying; all that is needed out here is to stop a flex or
     grid parent stretching the button to the full row. `start` is valid in
     both, and is ignored in a block parent, where the inline-flex button
     already sizes to its content. */
  .bmp-size-guide__trigger--relocated {
    align-self: start;
  }

  .bmp-size-guide__trigger-label {
    text-decoration: underline dashed rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 60%) 2px;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }

  .bmp-size-guide__trigger:hover .bmp-size-guide__trigger-label {
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .bmp-size-guide__trigger-icon svg {
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
  }

  /* Editor-only missing-block warning. Rendered only under
     request.design_mode, and CSS-suppressed by a [data-bmp-size-guide-config]
     marker, which the block emits ONLY on its dialog — i.e. only when the
     product actually has the configured option. On a product without it the
     block renders its editor note and deliberately no marker, which is safe
     because this warning is not rendered on that page either: the trigger
     self-gates on the same option name, so there is no legend to warn in.
     That makes the `:not(.quick-add-modal *)` clause below load-bearing rather
     than defensive — a design-mode quick-add clone is the one way a foreign
     product's marker can reach this document. */
  .bmp-size-guide__editor-warning {
    display: none;
  }

  /* :not(.quick-add-modal *): a design-mode quick-add popup on a collection
     template clones a product page in; its Size legend must not claim the
     COLLECTION template is missing a block it never carries. */
  body:not(:has([data-bmp-size-guide-config])) .bmp-size-guide__editor-warning:not(.quick-add-modal *) {
    display: inline;
    margin-inline-start: var(--gap-2xs);
    font-size: var(--font-size--2xs);
    font-style: italic;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  /* NOTE: there is deliberately NO `.quick-add-modal .bmp-size-guide__trigger
     { display: none }` rule here. Suppressing the trigger inside the popup is
     what the first quick-add fix did, and it removed the size guide from the
     surface where quick-add is the only way to reach it. The nesting hazard it
     was defending against (a dialog opened while a DOM descendant of the open
     quick-add dialog: click/Escape bubbling into the outer component's
     handlers per dialog.js:62-63 + isClickedOutside's geometric test at
     utilities.js:395-401, and `.quick-add-modal`'s forwards-filled transform
     becoming the containing block of the nested fixed-position dialog) is now
     fixed at the root by bmp-size-guide-portal.js, which reparents the dialog
     to <body> before it can be opened. Re-adding a suppression rule here would
     silently delete the feature again — scripts/check-size-guide-isolation.mjs
     fails the build if one comes back. */
{% endstylesheet %}
